#
# t-IX built by tiny-build, under t-IX: this file is to be /src/Buildfile
# on tiny-kernel's image, where /src is tiny/; for now it is tried on the
# host (docs/plans/plan_tiny_buildfile.md, its steps).
#
#     cd /src
#     build build          tiny-build itself, as /src/build, to run
#     build rc             the shell; any program by its name in /bin
#     build                every program, the kernel, the image
#
# It is also meant to be read: how t-IX's pieces fit together, from the
# bottom up. A program stands on its own units, on the libraries
# several programs share, on the standard library (TinyLibCore/), and
# on the runtimes, what a program links besides its own code; the
# kernel stands on the same library and runtime; the image is the
# kernel and all the rest, as files.
#
# What a recipe makes is beside its source: TinyShell.o and its
# interface TinyShell.tmi beside TinyShell.ml, TinyC/TinyCParser.o in
# TinyC/; a program at the top, by its name (rc, build, ml). `build
# clean` removes them.
#
# The tools are tiny-kernel's: ml (tiny-ml), cc (tiny-c), as
# (tiny-assembler), ld (tiny-linker). On the host they are tiny-ml and
# so on: a directory of links named ml, cc, as and ld first in the PATH
# runs this file there too (tiny-build runs a recipe with sh on the
# host, rc on tiny-kernel: a recipe here is a command both read, but
# the image's, which is rc's).
#
# coupling: TinyKernel/Makefile says the same, its lists (CORE, UNITS_x
# a program's units, I_x a unit's interfaces, MEM_x, KUNITS, SRC): a
# change to one is a change to the other, until the Makefile is made
# from this file (the plan's last step).
#
# Its problems, today's tools', each a step of the plan:
# - ml finds no interface by itself: each unit's rule names the .tmi it
#   reads, in the order the Makefile gives them (the .tmi's bytes
#   depend on it); and a unit's .mli is not a prerequisite (ml takes
#   no .mli argument), so a changed interface does not remake the
#   units that read it: ml -I and ml -M, later;
# - no libraries: a link names every object of the library (LIBO).


# the programs, by their names in /bin; then the kernel
PROGS = sh rc emacs office troff play mltests paint windows square tetris \
    calc temp ping get put httpd utils ed bigmem www prof db vcs build as ld \
    cc ml trace debug

all: $PROGS kernel.img


#############################################################################
# How each kind of file is made
#############################################################################

# an ML unit: its interfaces (the prerequisites a unit's rule gives),
# then its source, -nostdlib (the library's interfaces are among them,
# LIB, not read from /lib/ml); its .tmi made with its .o
%.o: %.ml
	ml -c -nostdlib -o $target $prereq
%.tmi: %.o

# C, by tiny-c; assembly written by hand, by tiny-assembler (the
# compilers write their objects themselves)
%.o: %.c
	cc -o $target $stem.c
%.o: %.tm
	as -o $target $stem.tm


#############################################################################
# The runtimes: what a program links besides its own code
#############################################################################

# C's, on any system (runtime/): _start, which calls main, then exits,
# linked first since the machine starts at 0; the unsigned division
# tiny-c calls; libc, print and the strings. Then tiny-kernel's calls
# for C (TinyKernel/libc/: sys.tm, a sys instruction each; draw.c and
# net.c for the programs that draw or dial)
CRT = runtime/start.o runtime/udivmod.o runtime/libc.o TinyKernel/libc/sys.o

# ML's: runtime/ml.c, the allocator and Cheney's collector, #included by
# mlsys.c, which gives it tiny-kernel's memory and calls; end.tm, the
# image's end, where an ML program's value stack can start, linked last
MLRT = TinyLibSystem/tinykernel/mlsys.o
MLEND = TinyKernel/end.o
TinyLibSystem/tinykernel/mlsys.o: TinyLibSystem/tinykernel/mlsys.c runtime/ml.c TinyKernel/libc/user.h


#############################################################################
# The standard library: TinyLibCore/, OCaml's subset
#############################################################################

# Its units in their order, each compiled with the interfaces of the
# ones before it (as ocaml-light's stdlib is compiled in an order, its
# OBJS); every ML unit and main is compiled with all of them (LIB) and
# every ML program links them (LIBO). On the image they are /lib/ml.
CORE = Stdlib Char Seq List String Bytes Array Option Int32 Int64 Filename Buffer Printf Marshal Hashtbl Common Sha1 Fpath Fpath_ Exit In_channel Out_channel Chan Logs

LIB = ${CORE:%=TinyLibCore/%.tmi}
LIBO = ${CORE:%=TinyLibCore/%.o}

